
(A) Remark 5.5(a): Q' agrees with Q outside E, Q(E) = q.  Claim: Pr[Q' in M_N] >= 1 - delta - N q.
    The proof gives the sharper bound 1 - delta - (1 - (1-q)^N); both are compared with the exact value.
    6000 random cases (4 to 6 outcomes, N = 1..6, delta in {1/20, 1/10, 1/5, 1/2}); the bound 1 - delta - N q is positive in 5892;
    in 2318 of them q > 0 and Q' differs from Q
    cases with Pr[Q' in M_N] below the sharper bound: 0
    all cases: smallest value of Pr[Q' in M_N] - (1 - delta - (1 - (1-q)^N)) found: 0.039760
      there: N = 5, delta = 1/20, q = 0, Pr[Q' in M_N] = 0.989760, sharper bound = 0.950000, 1 - delta - N q = 0.950000
      Q  = ['0', '1/10', '1/10', '2/5', '2/5', '0']
      Q' = ['0', '1/10', '1/10', '2/5', '2/5', '0']
      tests = [(0,), (0,), (1, 2, 3, 5)] with weights ['1/4', '1/8', '1/2']
    cases with q > 0 and Q' different from Q: smallest value of Pr[Q' in M_N] - (1 - delta - (1 - (1-q)^N)) found: 0.044942
      there: N = 5, delta = 1/20, q = 1/100, Pr[Q' in M_N] = 0.945932, sharper bound = 0.900990, 1 - delta - N q = 0.900000
      Q  = ['1/1000', '9/1000', '297/1000', '99/250', '297/1000']
      Q' = ['1/100', '0', '297/1000', '99/250', '297/1000']
      tests = [(0, 1), (0,), (1,), (1, 3), (2, 4)] with weights ['1/17', '4/17', '2/17', '2/17', '8/17']
    the coupled run tau' has the law Q' in 2000 random cases: True

(B) Remark 5.5(a): the term N q is needed, and the bound is not far from the truth.
    Outcomes e1, e2 in E and o1, o2 outside.  Q(e1) = q, Q(e2) = 0; Q'(e1) = 0, Q'(e2) = q; Q = Q' outside E.
    Tests {e1} and {o1}.  Q' gives {e1} the probability 0, so Q' matches only if no recorded run is e1.
    weights 1/2, 1/2; N = 2, delta = 1/10, q = 1/40,  Pr[Q' in M_N] = 0.950625 <= (1-q)^N = 0.950625;  bounds: sharper 0.850625, 1 - delta - N q = 0.850000;  N q = 0.0500, Pr[Q' not in M_N] = 0.0494
    weights 1/2, 1/2; N = 5, delta = 1/10, q = 1/100, Pr[Q' in M_N] = 0.948679 <= (1-q)^N = 0.950990;  bounds: sharper 0.850990, 1 - delta - N q = 0.850000;  N q = 0.0500, Pr[Q' not in M_N] = 0.0513
    weights 1/2, 1/2; N = 6, delta = 1/5,  q = 1/20,  Pr[Q' in M_N] = 0.727054 <= (1-q)^N = 0.735092;  bounds: sharper 0.535092, 1 - delta - N q = 0.500000;  N q = 0.3000, Pr[Q' not in M_N] = 0.2729
    weights 1/2, 1/2; N = 6, delta = 1/20, q = 1/6,   Pr[Q' in M_N] = 0.331236 <= (1-q)^N = 0.334898;  bounds: sharper 0.284898, 1 - delta - N q = -0.050000;  N q = 1.0000, Pr[Q' not in M_N] = 0.6688
    weights 1/2, 1/2; N = 4, delta = 1/2,  q = 1/4,   Pr[Q' in M_N] = 0.289923 <= (1-q)^N = 0.316406;  bounds: sharper -0.183594, 1 - delta - N q = -0.500000;  N q = 1.0000, Pr[Q' not in M_N] = 0.7101
    In each row the imitator is excluded at least as often as a run meets E: with probability at least 1 - (1-q)^N,
    which is close to N q when N q is small, and 1 - e^(-c) in the limit for q = c/N.
    So 'much rarer than 1/N' cannot be replaced by 'of frequency about 1/N' for every behaviour.
    search over 6240 members of this family (weights 1/1024 and 1023/1024, N = 1..8): the largest value of
    Pr[Q' not in M_N] / (delta + 1 - (1-q)^N) is 0.9315, at N = 8, delta = 1/20, q = 1/20, Q(o1 | not E) = 13/20:
    Pr[Q' not in M_N] = 0.360088 against delta + 1 - (1-q)^N = 0.386580; never above 1.

(C) The example of Remark 5.5(a) on a scaffold whose inputs depend on the answers.
    z uniform on {0,1,2}.  Call 1 at input z.  Call 2 at input 5 (the rare kind) if z = 2 and the first answer
    is 1, else at input 3 + (first answer).  After a call at input 5 there is a third call, at input 6 + (second answer).
    14 complete runs; the human system meets the rare kind with probability q = 1/30
    imitator: answer 1 with probability 1    at input 5, and then with probability 1 or 1 (after the answer 0 or 1 there);  the laws agree outside E: True, Q'(E) = 1/30, TV(Q', Q) = 0.0283;  N = 2, delta = 1/10  Pr[Q' in M_N] = 0.999644 >= 0.834444 >= 0.833333
    imitator: answer 1 with probability 1    at input 5, and then with probability 1 or 1 (after the answer 0 or 1 there);  the laws agree outside E: True, Q'(E) = 1/30, TV(Q', Q) = 0.0283;  N = 3, delta = 1/5   Pr[Q' in M_N] = 0.974333 >= 0.703296 >= 0.700000
    imitator: answer 1 with probability 1    at input 5, and then with probability 1 or 1 (after the answer 0 or 1 there);  the laws agree outside E: True, Q'(E) = 1/30, TV(Q', Q) = 0.0283;  N = 4, delta = 1/10  Pr[Q' in M_N] = 0.992759 >= 0.773186 >= 0.766667
    imitator: answer 1 with probability 0    at input 5, and then with probability 0 or 1 (after the answer 0 or 1 there);  the laws agree outside E: True, Q'(E) = 1/30, TV(Q', Q) = 0.0200;  N = 2, delta = 1/10  Pr[Q' in M_N] = 0.960400 >= 0.834444 >= 0.833333
    imitator: answer 1 with probability 0    at input 5, and then with probability 0 or 1 (after the answer 0 or 1 there);  the laws agree outside E: True, Q'(E) = 1/30, TV(Q', Q) = 0.0200;  N = 3, delta = 1/5   Pr[Q' in M_N] = 0.941192 >= 0.703296 >= 0.700000
    imitator: answer 1 with probability 0    at input 5, and then with probability 0 or 1 (after the answer 0 or 1 there);  the laws agree outside E: True, Q'(E) = 1/30, TV(Q', Q) = 0.0200;  N = 4, delta = 1/10  Pr[Q' in M_N] = 0.916383 >= 0.773186 >= 0.766667
    imitator: answer 1 with probability 1/2  at input 5, and then with probability 9/10 or 1/10 (after the answer 0 or 1 there);  the laws agree outside E: True, Q'(E) = 1/30, TV(Q', Q) = 0.0150;  N = 2, delta = 1/10  Pr[Q' in M_N] = 0.999644 >= 0.834444 >= 0.833333
    imitator: answer 1 with probability 1/2  at input 5, and then with probability 9/10 or 1/10 (after the answer 0 or 1 there);  the laws agree outside E: True, Q'(E) = 1/30, TV(Q', Q) = 0.0150;  N = 3, delta = 1/5   Pr[Q' in M_N] = 0.974333 >= 0.703296 >= 0.700000
    imitator: answer 1 with probability 1/2  at input 5, and then with probability 9/10 or 1/10 (after the answer 0 or 1 there);  the laws agree outside E: True, Q'(E) = 1/30, TV(Q', Q) = 0.0150;  N = 4, delta = 1/10  Pr[Q' in M_N] = 0.990786 >= 0.773186 >= 0.766667
    in all nine rows: the two run laws agree outside E, Q'(E) = q, and the bound holds: True

(D) Remark 5.5(b): the numbers of the example.
    N = 1000, delta = 0.05, pi(R) = 1/2: a_N(R) = 0.00438, 1 - exp(-a_N(R)) = 0.00437, tol_N(R) = sqrt(a_N(R)/2) = 0.04681
    kl(0 || 0.02) = ln(1/0.98) = 0.02020 > a_N(R): an imitator that gives the test R the probability 0.02 does not match
    the same comparison in exact arithmetic: 0.98^1000 = 1.683e-09 < pi delta / 2 = 1/80 = 0.0125: True

(E) Remark 5.5(c): tests B1, B2 that never occurred, with E inside their union.
    Claim: every matching Q' has Q'(E) <= Q'(B1) + Q'(B2) <= (1 - e^(-a(B1))) + (1 - e^(-a(B2))) < a(B1) + a(B2).
    Checked exactly, as (1 - Q'(B_i))^N >= pi_i delta / 2, on the scaffold of block (C) with
    B1 = 'the answer at input 5 is 0', B2 = 'the answer at input 5 is 1' (their union is E), and on random cases.
    367 random matching candidates on that scaffold: Q'(E) < a(B1) + a(B2) in all: True; largest ratio Q'(E)/(a(B1)+a(B2)) = 0.8536
    N =  100, delta = 1/20, weights 1/2 and 1/2: a(B1) + a(B2) = 0.08764; largest Q'(B1) + Q'(B2) of a matching law = 0.08575
    N = 1000, delta = 1/20, weights 1/4 and 1/4: a(B1) + a(B2) = 0.01015; largest Q'(B1) + Q'(B2) of a matching law = 0.01012
    N = 3835, delta = 1/20, weights 1/1048576 and 1/1048576: a(B1) + a(B2) = 0.00915; largest Q'(B1) + Q'(B2) of a matching law = 0.00913

(F) Proposition 5.4 for the fair coin: one call at a uniform input among u, the human a uniformly random function.
    Claim printed in Section 8.2: the coin is matching with probability at least 1 - delta - N^2/(2u)
    (Proposition 5.4 has 1 - delta - C(N,2)/u).  Exact, over all 2^u functions and all u^N samples of inputs.
    u = 6, N = 2, delta = 1/10  Pr[the coin is matching] = 1.000000 >= 1 - delta - C(N,2)/u = 0.733333 >= 1 - delta - N^2/(2u) = 0.566667: True
    u = 6, N = 3, delta = 1/5   Pr[the coin is matching] = 0.966435 >= 1 - delta - C(N,2)/u = 0.300000 >= 1 - delta - N^2/(2u) = 0.050000: True
    u = 8, N = 2, delta = 1/20  Pr[the coin is matching] = 1.000000 >= 1 - delta - C(N,2)/u = 0.825000 >= 1 - delta - N^2/(2u) = 0.700000: True
    u = 8, N = 3, delta = 1/10  Pr[the coin is matching] = 0.998047 >= 1 - delta - C(N,2)/u = 0.525000 >= 1 - delta - N^2/(2u) = 0.337500: True

(G) Proposition 6.4: the collision statistic c = 1[y1 = y2] - A(y1|x) - A(y2|x) + sum_y A(y|x)^2.
    3000 random pairs of laws on q = 2..5 answers: E[c | x] = sum_y (P - A)^2 exactly: True; -1/2 <= c <= 2: True;
    TV(P, A) <= (sqrt(q)/2) sqrt(sum_y (P - A)^2): True (largest ratio of the squares 1.0000)
    so with D the mean of sum_y (P - A)^2 over a uniformly chosen position of a run of A (zero at positions the run
    does not reach): beta(A) <= (L/2) sqrt(q D) by the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, as Proposition 6.4(c) says.
    m = 1e+04 rounds, delta = 0.05, q =  2: with average statistic 0 the certificate reads beta(A)/L <= (1/2) sqrt(q * 0.03060) = 0.1237
    m = 1e+04 rounds, delta = 0.05, q = 10: with average statistic 0 the certificate reads beta(A)/L <= (1/2) sqrt(q * 0.03060) = 0.2766
    m = 1e+06 rounds, delta = 0.05, q =  2: with average statistic 0 the certificate reads beta(A)/L <= (1/2) sqrt(q * 0.00306) = 0.0391
    m = 1e+06 rounds, delta = 0.05, q = 10: with average statistic 0 the certificate reads beta(A)/L <= (1/2) sqrt(q * 0.00306) = 0.0875
    m = 1e+08 rounds, delta = 0.05, q =  2: with average statistic 0 the certificate reads beta(A)/L <= (1/2) sqrt(q * 0.00031) = 0.0124
    m = 1e+08 rounds, delta = 0.05, q = 10: with average statistic 0 the certificate reads beta(A)/L <= (1/2) sqrt(q * 0.00031) = 0.0277

(H) Arithmetic of sentences added to the note.
    Section 8.3: sqrt((2 K ln 2 + 2 ln(2/delta))/N) < 1 needs N > 2 K ln 2 + 2 ln(2/delta); for K = 1e9 bits: 2 K ln 2 = 1.386e+09 runs
    Section 8.3: if TV(Q_nu, Q) >= 1/3 the right side of Pr4 is at least 1 for that nu, whatever N is: 3 * (1/3) = 1
    Section 8.3: r(nu) = sqrt((K_nu ln 2 + ln(2/delta))/(2N)) = sqrt(a/2) with a = (ln(1/w(nu)) + ln(2/delta))/N: the error of Pr3 for a test of weight w(nu)
    Section 8.6, Pr2(b): u =    5, K =   2: n <= min{u/4, (K-2)/log2(4u+2)} = 0; least value of the formula for these n: 0.6818 (> 0.51: True)
    Section 8.6, Pr2(b): u =    5, K =  20: n <= min{u/4, (K-2)/log2(4u+2)} = 1; least value of the formula for these n: 0.7273 (> 0.51: True)
    Section 8.6, Pr2(b): u =    8, K =  30: n <= min{u/4, (K-2)/log2(4u+2)} = 2; least value of the formula for these n: 0.7059 (> 0.51: True)
    Section 8.6, Pr2(b): u = 1024, K = 200: n <= min{u/4, (K-2)/log2(4u+2)} = 16; least value of the formula for these n: 0.9800 (> 0.51: True)
    Section 8.6, Pr2(b): closing the class as in Corollary 4.6 with weights (C_R - 1) : 1 multiplies the value by (C_R - 1)/C_R = 1 - 1/C_R
    Section 8.6, Pr2(c): (3/4)(16/17)(10/11)(4/5) = 0.51337 > 0.51
